# TODO - Portable capability IR and platform isolation

## Objective

Eliminate platform and device coupling throughout the workspace. Adding an
operating system or backend must extend shared capabilities for all titles
without per-title ports. Use the typed capability IR described in
[the platform design](../../../technical/platform-ir.md).

## Acceptance

- Migrate every existing occurrence in the portability baseline to the
  owning platform adapter or a neutral shared contract. Delete the baseline
  once empty; never increase it, hide occurrences or weaken the gate.
- Centralise target selection and native SDK dependencies in explicitly
  owned adapter/composition directories. No OS, architecture, vendor or
  backend-specific branching or native handles in other authored code,
  including titles, importers, shared libraries, tools and inline tests.
- Reject direct host I/O outside its owning adapter, including filesystem,
  sockets, clocks, environment, process launch and native device access.
  Use narrow injected service ports; keep pure data and algorithms portable.
- Reuse typed commands, events, observations and errors for graphics,
  presentation, audio, input, haptics, storage, networking, timing,
  lifecycle, host services and capability discovery. Extend contracts for
  real consumers rather than introducing parallel or speculative systems.
- Normalise physical controller identities, axes, buttons, touch, pointer
  input, sensors and optional haptic actuators. Persist device-independent
  per-player settings; support explicit capability absence, safe disconnect
  and focus loss, bounded feedback and no gameplay changes on failure.
- Share menu, presentation, adaptive widescreen, audio, input, saves and
  other common mechanisms across all titles. Keep title-specific scene
  requirements and gameplay in the title. Verify existing title behaviour
  whenever shared contracts or adapters change.
- Enforce the isolation in `cargo validate` over every authored source and
  manifest, including target tables, aliases, features, re-exports, macros,
  imports, direct native dependency paths and nested directories. Use
  parsed structures and dependency metadata where lexical checks are
  insufficient. Add positive and negative controls with no original media.
- Test each portable contract using deterministic fake adapters, capability
  absence, resource budgets, event ordering and error paths. Verify
  equivalent observable behaviour across implemented backends.
- Preserve the existing Linux runtime and packaging; validate shared
  contracts against Windows, Android and Web targets where available.
  Implement missing adapters via the build roadmap. Record any unavailable
  toolchains or physical hardware; do not claim untested compatibility.

## Evidence

Commit 569801d introduced the first haptic capability contract and a
mandatory incremental portability gate. Its initial inventory records
253 occurrences across 27 files. Fresh validation passed in run
1795635890959083883-1411397. The gate prevents additional matching
couplings above the recorded ceilings, but strict zero-debt isolation,
semantic coverage, native haptics and cross-target support remain open.

The Linux input adapter now implements the shared haptic capability using
the same admitted controller identity as input. SDL reports support for
the standard two-channel command interface, not the actual actuator
count. Finite effects replace existing rumble; unfocused, unsupported
and detached devices reject new effects. Focus loss, hiding, minimizing,
close, detach and adapter drop request a stop when a device is available.
A failed cleanup cannot extend the bounded effect duration. Synthetic
delivery tests cover normalized intensity, maximum duration, fallback,
explicit stop and backend rejection. The native rendering smoke also
rejects feedback for an unknown device. Fresh validation with Xvfb,
native rendering and dummy audio passed in run
1795636187133956702-1494963. Physical haptics, title feedback integration
and non-Linux adapters remain unverified.

Commit 103b1e4 resolves canonical SDK package identities in direct and
workspace-inherited dependency aliases, including target-specific and
nested dependency groups. Tests cover aliases, normalized spellings
and missing workspace references. The same fresh joint gate above
passed without increasing the migration baseline.

Storage, title launch and package tests now share the existing atomic
private temporary directory mechanism through a storage adapter fixture.
The fixture is enabled for tests without requiring it in title releases.
A regression test verifies owner-only permissions, independent roots and
cleanup without affecting another root. Four Unix API occurrences across
two consumer files were removed from the migration baseline. The joint
fresh workspace gate passed in run 1795636430293093179-1561568.

The native title build and licence inventory now use the same typed Cargo
request in the dependency-free host platform adapter. Host support,
process execution and artifact lookup are owned by that adapter. The
title registry still selects the authored package and feature. Tests
reject option-like identifiers and verify target, feature and artifact
path consistency. Three target condition occurrences were removed from
the baseline. The joint fresh native gate passed in run
1795636790890770112-1650625. No additional target toolchain is implemented.

The shared physical input contract now admits six controller axes, 21
controller buttons and five pointer buttons independent of native enum
values. The keyboard contract uses stable routing identities and the
portable digital profile parser accepts an injected legacy key-name
resolver. Existing version-one names and routing codes are preserved;
legacy extended keyboard identities remain an adapter translation concern.
Native mapping tests, pure profile admission tests and title remapping
coverage passed with native rendering and dummy audio in fresh validation
1795636790890770112-1650625. Six title-side platform references were
removed from the baseline. Physical devices and other backends remain
unverified.

The host-independence gate now parses Rust token trees for shared,
runtime and importer roles. It rejects directly named host modules,
root aliases and globs, grouped mixed-module imports, visible macro-body
paths and output macros. Explicit in-memory I/O and fixed duration types
remain admitted. The manifest codec now imports only its host-free I/O
types and traits. Synthetic controls verify these distinctions. Fresh
validation passed in run 1795637004022076798-1711638. No baseline entries
were added. This is not semantic name resolution or macro expansion;
title and tool host-I/O migration remains open.

Frame layout, rendering errors, sprite command values, scene budgets and
bounded event outcomes now live in backend-independent graphics modules.
The existing RGBA stride, extent and byte-budget checks are preserved;
texture creation, rectangle conversion and native event polling remain
in the adapter. Title and reference-rendering consumers use the neutral
values directly, removing twelve further platform references. Fresh
native render/audio validation passed in run
1795637163797669035-1780490. Other backend implementations and physical
performance acceptance remain open.
